#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139643 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to factor the following trinomial:\n$t^4+4t^2+3$.\n\nThis trinomial is a quartic, but it can also be viewed as a quadratic \"in disguise.\" To see this, notice that if we introduce a new variable $z=t^2$, then we can rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{4} + 4t^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n(t^{2})^{2} + 4(t^{2}) + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= z^{2} + 4z + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} + 4z + 3 &=  \\\\\n(z + 1)(z + 3) &=  \\\\\n&= (t^{2} + 1)(t^{2} + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $t^4+4t^2+3$ factors into $(t^2+1)(t^2+3)$. We can double-check this result by multiplying it out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(t^{2} + 1)(t^{2} + 3) &=  \\\\\nt^{2}(t^{2} + 3) + 1(t^{2} + 3) &=  \\\\\nt^{4} + 3t^{2} + t^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= t^{4} + 4t^{2} + 3 \\mid ✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7d740bed-d490-55c4-b311-075223236921" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2961 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
